1
00:00:07,223 --> 00:00:10,888
Let's create an empty .dyno file.

2
00:00:10,888 --> 00:00:15,987
Double-clicking on it opens two views.

3
00:00:15,987 --> 00:00:18,488
The model is written on the left.

4
00:00:18,488 --> 00:00:21,994
On the right, dyno shows what it understood. It updates while typing.

5
00:00:21,994 --> 00:00:24,628
Let's start with a parameter: the discount factor beta.

6
00:00:24,628 --> 00:00:28,144
The arrow <- is an assignment: it gives a value to a symbol.

7
00:00:28,144 --> 00:00:31,984
A second parameter, the gross interest rate, defined from beta.

8
00:00:31,984 --> 00:00:35,493
Assignments are evaluated in order, so r can use beta.

9
00:00:35,493 --> 00:00:47,931
Now the equations.

10
00:00:47,931 --> 00:00:51,934
Equations use the equal sign. Time is written in brackets: t for today, t-1 for yesterday.

11
00:00:51,934 --> 00:00:55,937
There is no list of variables to declare: dyno works it out from the equations.

12
00:00:55,937 --> 00:01:04,525
It also noticed that alpha and delta have no value yet.

13
00:01:04,525 --> 00:01:13,823
Future values are denoted by t+1.

14
00:01:13,823 --> 00:01:25,318
Productivity follows a log-normal AR(1) process, with mean 1.

15
00:01:25,318 --> 00:01:29,321
The shock is declared by its distribution: normal, with a standard deviation of 1%.

16
00:01:29,321 --> 00:01:32,323
dyno classifies epsilon as exogenous on its own.

17
00:01:32,323 --> 00:01:42,382
The system is complete. Equations can be tagged with their name.

18
00:01:42,382 --> 00:01:51,723
Arbitrary information can also be attached: here, a tag and a label.

19
00:01:51,723 --> 00:01:55,245
The names show up in the report.

20
00:01:55,245 --> 00:02:05,366
The tilde stands for the steady state. Let's type rough guesses.

21
00:02:05,366 --> 00:02:12,471
Are these guesses right? Let's ask dyno to check the model.

22
00:02:12,471 --> 00:02:16,479
They are not: the residuals of the equations are not zero.

23
00:02:16,479 --> 00:02:21,864
Rather than solving the steady state by hand, dyno can find it, before the check.

24
00:02:21,864 --> 00:02:29,630
The steady state converged, and now all residuals are zero.

25
00:02:29,630 --> 00:02:39,007
The steady-state card tells how it was found: algorithm, iterations, final residual.

26
00:02:39,007 --> 00:02:47,375
The residuals card lists the residual of each equation.

27
00:02:47,375 --> 00:02:51,382
The check also looks at the eigenvalues: the Blanchard-Kahn conditions are met.

28
00:02:51,382 --> 00:02:57,440
What if humans were really patient?

29
00:02:57,440 --> 00:03:01,949
With beta = 1.08, the Blanchard-Kahn conditions are not met: too many stable roots.

30
00:03:01,949 --> 00:03:07,627
Just kidding.

31
00:03:07,627 --> 00:03:19,206
@run lines say what to do with the model, in order: here, solve and simulate.

32
00:03:19,206 --> 00:03:23,214
simulate computes impulse responses to the shock: here they are, as a table.

33
00:03:23,214 --> 00:03:35,123
Commands take options, in YAML syntax: here, a horizon of 100 periods.

34
00:03:35,123 --> 00:03:40,005
Now a plot.

35
00:03:40,005 --> 00:03:44,014
Here are the impulse responses to a productivity shock.

36
00:03:44,014 --> 00:03:52,321
plot takes options too: the variables to show, and the units.

37
00:03:52,321 --> 00:03:56,329
Responses are now in log-deviations from the steady state, in percent.

38
00:03:56,329 --> 00:04:00,770
Change a parameter, and the whole analysis reruns.

39
00:04:00,770 --> 00:04:07,796
A less persistent shock: capital now peaks after 3 periods, instead of 8.

40
00:04:07,796 --> 00:04:14,942
A very persistent one: capital keeps building up for 20 periods.

41
00:04:14,942 --> 00:04:18,949
With slower depreciation, it builds up for 35 periods.

42
00:04:18,949 --> 00:04:24,171
Back to the original values.

43
00:04:24,171 --> 00:04:32,553
To compare calibrations, list them in a variants line, before the other commands.

44
00:04:32,553 --> 00:04:36,055
Each plot now shows both calibrations.

45
00:04:36,055 --> 00:04:44,473
Finally, a semicolon mutes a command: let's keep only the plot.

46
00:04:44,473 --> 00:04:50,197
And one more calibration, for good measure.

47
00:04:50,197 --> 00:04:54,206
That's a complete model, from an empty file, in about twenty-five lines.

48
00:04:54,206 --> 00:05:02,203
Thank you for watching!
